Lets say you have 6 terms open, I usually have 10 sitting around in
screen so this isn't unreal.  And you want to log in via ssh at some
other computer.  With screen all you have to once you log in is
'screen -d -rr'.  Now the screen session that was running in one xterm
is now running in the same pty you are in.  You can log out completely
and log back in and relaunch the same screen session you left running
detached from a tty/pty.  What does this mean?  It means one
window/screen/console that has all of your console apps sitting
exactly in the same state you left them in.

I get on computer in moring.  Check email and one or two news groups
and go to work.  I leave screen and all the apps running in it just
sitting there.

I get to work and log in via http://netspace.org/ssh on one of the
break room computers.  After logging in I 'screen -d -rr' and I am
right where I left off at home.  No need to launch email or slrn, they
are already there where I left them along with all my other apps.

When it is time for work to start I just kill the wondow and get to
work.  Then when break time comes I go back and log in again and
reattach the screen session and everything is exactly were it was
before.

Rinse and repeat till I get home and the whole thing starts over
again.

Basically I never have to launch
email/news/txt_browser/editor/rss/shell other than the first time I
log in after a reboot.  Even though I am logging in 4 times a day I
never loose a moment of productivity.

